import{_ as i,P as a,d as n,e as r,y as s,q as o,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.2 HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.2 HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1027,"xkLessons":{"A.2":{"title":"A.2 HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces (Complete Interface Card)","lead":"HV02 separates formal boundaries, actual passage, and causal effects of specified channels; neither membership nor influence scope automatically becomes consent or jurisdiction.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3720–3785; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-02#section-a-2. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research manuscripts and excerpts remain available for download and checking against the original edition.","Short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “membership equals consent” is a prohibited leap. HV02 does not merely draw an inside/outside line; it registers members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and cross-boundary interfaces together. Formal rosters, actual resource control, technical openness, and real passage may differ; relevant judgments should pause when these are conflated or exclusions are invisible. [T-main]","The upstream inferential dependency is HV01, but inputs may be HV01 candidate objects or supported structural domains, carrying their evidence status. R0 does not require G1 to have passed in advance, so “grouping gain has not yet been established” does not prevent describing thresholds, waiting, and refusals; nor does it permit silently rewriting a candidate object as a unified community. [T-main; T-HV01]","HV02-R0-boundary-inventory permits description of boundary states, interface accessibility, gatekeeping positions, refusal records, and exclusion risks when the six boundary contents and formal–actual differences can be registered item by item. Its ceiling is inventory and description only; it cannot claim that the boundary has produced a causal selection effect. “Selective openness” as a state differs in strength from an established causal effect. [T-main]","HV02-R1-selective-effect requires a preregistered boundary or interface change that, through a qualified G2-instance, shows an above-threshold channel effect on specified cross-boundary flows or admission/refusal outcomes, with CAUSAL and E4 additionally required. Permitted conclusions are limited to the specified channel, window, positions, and distribution of cross-boundary costs; spatial, organizational, or influence scope cannot imply J-axis authority to act. Observed before-and-after change still requires addressing competing explanations and matched comparisons. [T-main; T-G2]","A boundary is not closure. An interface may be open to some information, impose eligibility restrictions on other resources, and have different actual accessibility for different groups; boundaries of rights and responsibilities need not coincide with physical walls. Nine-axis elevation requires adding membership categories, cross-domain interfaces, representative authorization, and inherited protections; especially after gatekeeping nodes change, recheck who can refuse and who can appeal. [T-main]","States, observables, and evidence jointly help locate gaps: roster exceptions, usage records, flows, refusal reasons, waiting times, and formal–actual differences each answer different questions. For positions unable to access, unable to exit, or withheld for protection, “no usage record” cannot imply absence of need or existing consent. [T-main]","Outputs connect to HV05 uptake, HV07 write-back, and PF-9 exit, but the existence of an interface does not establish arrival of resources, write-back of bad news, or safe exit. Authorized rollback must correct membership/refusal records, actually restore corresponding admission or interface states, revoke erroneous actions, and verify completion; merely changing “closed” to “open” in a table is insufficient. [T-main]","The OECD’s urban accessibility method distinguishes the presence of a stop from accessibility of service opportunities, providing a local reference for the difference between formal openness and actual accessibility; it has not performed a preregistered G2 here, and a methodological definition cannot count as causal support for a boundary change. The original 39-field card is preserved in full below, with interpretation limited by the selected route. [P-OECD; T-main]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card, translated: HV02. Explanation: this is a short identifier within the card collection, tracking the same interface; it does not indicate that a real object has been established. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card, translated: human_variable:HV02. Explanation: the qualified namespace prevents name collisions; references to this ID must still carry the object and evidence status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card, translated: Boundaries and Interfaces. Explanation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for specific people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card, translated: Human boundaries must register members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and cross-boundary interfaces together. Explanation: this proposition must be understood together with the conditional routes and ceilings below; unconditional causal or normative conclusions cannot be extracted from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card, translated: Human structures involving inclusion, exclusion, exchange, or jurisdiction. Explanation: the scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean all objects within it have empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card, translated: Formal and actual boundaries are conflated, or exclusions are invisible. Explanation: pausing applies to judgments or adoption dependent on this; independently established descriptions may remain, but invalid premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card, translated: 1. human_variable:HV01. Explanation: an HV01 reference may carry candidate status; this card’s R0 does not require G1 to have passed. An upstream candidate reference must not be elevated by concealing its evidence status. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card: 1. E1; 2. EVIDENCE; 3. SOURCE. Interpretation: protocol dependencies constrain evidence gathering and expression; completing protocols does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card, translated: 1. route_id=HV02-R0-boundary-inventory;; claim_level=descriptive_classification;; when=Members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, cross-boundary interfaces, and formal–actual boundary differences can be registered item by item.;; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);; additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);; allowed_conclusion=Describe boundary states, interface accessibility, gatekeeping positions, refusal records, and exclusion risks.;; result_ceiling=Boundary and interface inventory only; must not claim that the boundary has produced a causal selection effect.; 2. route_id=HV02-R1-selective-effect;; claim_level=conditional_effect;; when=A preregistered boundary or interface change, through a qualified G2-instance, shows an above-threshold channel effect on specified cross-boundary flows or admission or refusal outcomes.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register boundary selection effects and the distribution of cross-boundary costs at the specified channel, window, and positions.;; result_ceiling=Limited to tested channels and outcomes; spatial, organizational, or influence scope must not imply J-axis jurisdiction and authority to act. Explanation: R0 only inventories boundaries and interfaces; R1 requires a qualified preregistered G2 channel effect plus CAUSAL and E4. Sharing a task object does not give the two routes equal conclusion strength. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inferences (allowed_inference). Original card, translated: 1. Boundary selectivity; 2. Interface accessibility; 3. Cross-boundary costs. Explanation: boundary selectivity, accessibility, and costs may be described under R0; a claim of causal effect for a specified channel requires R1, and this short list alone cannot bypass the routes. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leaps (prohibited_leap). Original card, translated: 1. Boundary equals closure; 2. Membership equals consent; 3. Influence scope equals jurisdiction. Explanation: these are common overreaches that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; check actual inference and subsequent use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card, translated: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: single cross-boundary events, individual interface-use cases, populations of membership categories, admission/refusal distributions, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: physical entrances, organizational boundaries, digital interfaces, jurisdictions, and cross-domain channels; T=time span: boundary effective periods, interface waiting, migration lags, and reorganization cycles; O=organizational level: user roles, gatekeeping teams, organizations, institutions, through to governance ecosystems; C=causal level: cross-boundary events, gatekeeping interaction mechanisms, interface structures, institutional rules, and system conditions; R=observation resolution: raw admission/refusal records, usage sequences, individual cases, flow distributions, service indicators, and summaries, recording compression losses; I=influence scope: direct users, excluded people, positions receiving indirect benefits or bearing costs, second-order spillovers, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network topology scope: interface nodes, gatekeeping bottlenecks, alternative paths, and cross-domain connections; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for inclusion, exclusion, interface changes, and adjustments to rights and responsibilities; elevation must record differences on each axis. Explanation: register all nine axes individually; in particular, influence scope I does not produce jurisdictional permission J, and compression losses must be retained when observation resolution R decreases. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card, translated: Boundaries that produce selectivity in flows of resources, information, rights, or responsibilities. Explanation: the original card says boundaries producing selectivity; under R0, treat these as objects to describe or test, without using the name to declare a causal selection effect in advance. [T-main]","18 | Scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card, translated: 1. Inside/outside positions; 2. Cross-boundary channels; 3. Boundaries of rights and responsibilities. Explanation: invariants are comparative responsibilities to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values remain unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required scale additions (required_scale_additions). Original card, translated: 1. New membership categories; 2. Cross-domain interfaces; 3. Representation and authorization; 4. Inherited protections. Explanation: elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. When materials about new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, limit conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Changing semantics (changing_semantics). Original card, translated: 1. Boundaries of membership, interfaces, and actual control may change. Explanation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; rebuild correspondences rather than assuming the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Non-applicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card, translated: 1. Nonhuman boundaries without concepts of membership, rights, or responsibilities. Explanation: non-applicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experience interfaces; they do not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card, translated: 1. Expansion of spatial or organizational scope automatically produces jurisdiction. Explanation: inference from local to whole needs separate support; broad influence, grand names, or formal similarity cannot supply missing representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card, translated: 1. Open; 2. Selectively open; 3. Closed; 4. Disputed; 5. Reorganizing. Explanation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require a window, grounds, and version. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card, translated: 1. Decisions about membership, admission, and exit; 2. Cross-boundary flows of resources, information, rights, and responsibilities; 3. Gatekeeping nodes, waiting times, and refusal reasons; 4. Differences between formal boundaries and boundaries of actual passage. Explanation: observable leads indicate where to seek materials; a single lead is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card, translated: 1. Membership rosters and exceptions; 2. Interface usage records; 3. Cross-boundary flow and refusal records. Explanation: obtain evidence according to route and task relevance; distinguish unknown, uncollected, not applicable, and unavailable for protection, without forcing disclosure to fill the table. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface contents (input_dependencies). Original card, translated: 1. HV01 candidate objects or supported structural domains and their evidence status; 2. Roles and authorization; R0 does not require G1 to have passed in advance. Explanation: this specifies what comes from upstream and its qualifications; gaps cannot silently disappear during transfer between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flows (output_effects). Original card, translated: 1. HV05 uptake; 2. HV07 write-back; 3. PF-9 exit. Explanation: variable flows indicate which downstream judgments may depend on this interface; passing a reference does not guarantee downstream conclusions, actual repair, or authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card, translated: Record lags in boundary effectiveness, change, exit, and appeal. Explanation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may happen at different times; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extension beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card, translated: Record informal boundaries, proxy access, and drift in digital spaces. Explanation: tie uncertainty to specific materials and positions; a single overall confidence score must not conceal positions that were never covered. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card, translated: Positions unable to access an interface, unable to exit, or withheld from public disclosure for protection. Explanation: exclusion zones limit the coverage of current inferences; they do not erase these people or facts from the world, and safety-related unavailability does not mean consent or absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card, translated: 1. Members; 2. Applicants; 3. Excluded people; 4. Those bearing burdens outside the boundary. Explanation: register both directly and indirectly affected people; visible members do not represent everyone bearing costs. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers (carrier). Original card, translated: 1. Membership rules; 2. Access mechanisms; 3. Legal or institutional boundaries; 4. Technical interfaces. Explanation: carriers are the structures or processes actually carrying something; the carrier’s name, completed construction, and delivery of required functions are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties (responsible_subject). Original card, translated: 1. Boundary setters; 2. Interface operators; 3. Authorizers. Explanation: specify who proposes the judgment and who operates or uses it; registering responsibility does not automatically adjudicate legal liability or issue new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card, translated: Separate boundary facts from the justification of boundaries. Explanation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the card. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings, and normative reasons do not automatically establish empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card, translated: Up to diagnostic level when interface and impact evidence are sufficient. Explanation: “up to diagnostic level when evidence is sufficient” is an overall upper bound; R0 still stops at inventory description, and diagnosis cannot issue permission to change admission or resource flows. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card, translated: This variable generates only descriptions of boundary states, interface barriers, exclusion risks, and testing needs; it does not authorize changes to admission, exit, rights, or resource flows. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Explanation: the interface itself produces no real-world authority to act; actual actions require separate checks of choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protections, and time limits. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card, translated: 1. Formal membership boundaries run contrary to actual resource-control boundaries; 2. A digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from entering. Explanation: use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or collecting evidence again, but do not automatically invalidate every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card, translated: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, affected people inside and outside the boundary may challenge inclusion, exclusion, and interface barriers through safely accessible anti-retaliation channels and trigger review independent of the original boundary decision chain. Explanation: appeals must be safely accessible, free of retaliation, and able to trigger independent review; a name alone does not mean there is power to correct errors. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card, translated: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, a designated responsible person must correct membership and refusal records within the specified time, actually restore affected admission rights or interface states, revoke erroneous boundary actions, and preserve versions and completion verification. Explanation: within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion; changing document wording does not mean rights and resources have been restored to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix scope using an HV01 candidate or supported object, retaining its evidence status.","Register formal–actual differences item by item for members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and interfaces.","R0 first describes thresholds and exclusions; an inventory must not be treated as a causal effect.","R1 separately uses preregistered G2 and CAUSAL/E4 to test specified channels and outcomes.","When transferring scale, check new members, cross-domain interfaces, representative authority, and inherited protections.","Correct boundaries through safe independent appeals; once authorized, restore actual interfaces and downstream records."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Formal boundary","leftText":"How rules or rosters specify it.","rightTitle":"Actual boundary","rightText":"How resources and passage are controlled in practice."},{"leftTitle":"R0 inventory description","leftText":"Describes who can reach, who is denied, and costs.","rightTitle":"R1 channel effect","rightText":"Preregistered G2 supports the causal effect of a specified change."},{"leftTitle":"Membership","leftText":"Registration in a particular member set.","rightTitle":"Consent","rightText":"Requires separate checking of the specific matter and genuine choice."},{"leftTitle":"Range of impact I","leftText":"Where a boundary change has effects.","rightTitle":"Jurisdiction J","rightText":"Determined by independently valid authorization."}],"boundaries":["Original-card counterexample: formal membership boundaries oppose actual resource-control boundaries.","Original-card counterexample: a digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from entering.","Teaching counterexample: an account’s existence is taken to imply consent to every use of personal data.","Boundary in the original: spatial expansion does not automatically add authority for inclusion, exclusion, or resource disposition."],"questions":["Can HV02 be entered before HV01 has G1 support?","Does an open interface mean everyone can actually access it?","Does a change from 20% to 40% automatically support R1?","Is correcting only the roster sufficient for rollback?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/figures/hv02-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Formal and actual differences in urban-access indicators","href":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Formal and actual differences in urban-access indicators","text":"OECD Chapter 2 notes that having transport stops does not guarantee access to sufficient opportunities, and distinguishes distance-based criteria from time/transport-mode criteria.","sourceLabel":"OECD: Transport Bridging Divides, Chapter 2","sourceUrl":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html","sourceLocator":"Measuring access to opportunities; definitions of three indicators; web page lines 3869–3877","boundary":"Only a conceptual and measurement comparison for accessibility, not a G2 instance of this card or proof that an admission or jurisdictional arrangement is justified.","href":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.2","start_line":3811,"end_line":3876,"startLine":3811,"endLine":3876}],"outputId":"section-a-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an open URL and actual passage","text":"Everyone can open a fictional service’s webpage, but submission requires a particular credential and its sole supported language is incomprehensible to some users. R0 may list boundaries at webpage, eligibility, and language levels, and unknown affected positions. “Can open” cannot declare universal accessibility, nor can untested removal of a barrier be said to produce a particular effect.","assumptions":"A constructed service scenario, with no actual personal data or eligibility decisions.","boundary":"Describing barriers authorizes no admission changes; normative justification and causal effects require separate checks."},{"title":"Teaching construction: before/after figures are not automatically G2","text":"Before a constructed interface change, 20 of 100 applications succeed; afterward, 40 of 100 succeed. The observed success proportion rises from 20% to 40%, a 20-percentage-point difference. If applicant composition, demand, or other processes changed simultaneously, the difference cannot be attributed to the interface alone. Applying for R1 additionally requires an eligible preregistered G2 and rules for comparison, thresholds, windows, and evidence; there is no formal supported status here.","assumptions":"The numbers are entirely instructional; two sets of 100 do not guarantee the same population or a valid comparison.","boundary":"Correlation or before/after differences cannot replace a causal contract; changing barriers also requires independent authority."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: formal membership boundaries oppose actual resource-control boundaries.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-card counterexample: a digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from entering.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: an account’s existence is taken to imply consent to every use of personal data.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: spatial expansion does not automatically add authority for inclusion, exclusion, or resource disposition.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can HV02 be entered before HV01 has G1 support?","answer":"A candidate-object reference may enter R0 while retaining boundaries and uncertainty.","basis":"input_dependencies and R0."},{"question":"Does an open interface mean everyone can actually access it?","answer":"No; check physical, language, safety, and eligibility barriers and actual interface records.","basis":"Counterexamples and observables."},{"question":"Does a change from 20% to 40% automatically support R1?","answer":"No; before/after differences additionally require proportionate G2 causal comparisons and preregistration rules.","basis":"R1 conditions and CAUSAL."},{"question":"Is correcting only the roster sufficient for rollback?","answer":"Actually restore affected admission/interfaces within valid scope, correct records, and verify completion.","basis":"rollback。"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/figures/hv02-routes.png","alt":"R0 boundary inventories require no prior G1 passage; R1 requires G2 specified-channel effects and CAUSAL/E4; neither route grants disposition authority.","caption":"Based on the complete conditional routes in A.2."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/figures/hv02-interfaces.png","alt":"Check formal and actual boundaries separately for cross-boundary interfaces of membership, resources, information, rights, and responsibilities; vulnerable absent positions and rollback effects cannot be omitted.","caption":"Based on zones C–E of A.2."}],"diagramBrief":"Two diagrams separately show route distinctions and six types of boundary interfaces; openness/closure is not ranked as good/bad. First check particular objects and consequences.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual G2-instance; teaching before/after proportions show only attribution gaps.","Specific boundary justification, jurisdictional authority, effects on excluded people, and actual restoration require field materials and independent procedural materials."],"taskId":"S-A-2","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.2, Chinese source lines 3634–3699","claim_support":["All 39 HV02 fields, R0 description/R1 channel effects, and ceilings."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-HV01","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.1, Chinese source lines 3568–3633","claim_support":["Candidate references may pass downstream with status retained."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["G2 specified-channel effects and their limits."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-OECD","title":"OECD: Transport Bridging Divides, Chapter 2","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/transport-bridging-divides_55ae1fd8-en/full-report/component-5.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2020 report; read 2026-10-14","locator":"Measuring access to opportunities; definitions of three indicators; web page lines 3869–3877","claim_support":["A stop’s existence differs from access to opportunities; distance indicators and access within given times/modes are defined separately."],"limits":["The researcher read selected portions of this section. They prove no causal effect of a boundary change and do not measure each user’s language, cost, bodily, or safety barriers."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.2 HV02 Boundaries and interfaces (complete interface card); Chinese source lines 3720–3785; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/source/S-A-2.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"b8376dd45ed34b89ab0433feffe70b653b9571d62e440add9fa50c8198f3709e","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-02.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-02.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-02.md"};function l(u,e,p,f,h,m){const t=a("SectionLesson");return n(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.2 HV02 Boundaries and Interfaces (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-2}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV02</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV02</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Boundaries and Interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>For human boundaries, record members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, and cross-boundary interfaces together.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Human structures with inclusion, exclusion, exchange, or jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Formal and actual boundaries are conflated or exclusions are invisible</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>1. <code>human_variable:HV01</code></td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. E1<br>2. EVIDENCE<br>3. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV02-R0-boundary-inventory;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=Members, resources, information, rights, responsibilities, cross-boundary interfaces, and differences between formal and actual boundaries can be recorded item by item;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Describe boundary states, interface accessibility, gatekeeping positions, records of refusal, and exclusion risks;<br>result_ceiling=An inventory of boundaries and interfaces only. Do not assert that the boundary has produced a causal selective effect.<br>2. route_id=HV02-R1-selective-effect;<br>claim_level=conditional_effect;<br>when=An eligible G2-instance shows that a pre-registered change to a boundary or interface has a channel effect above the threshold on the specified outcomes for cross-boundary flow, admission, or refusal;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register the boundary’s selective effect and the distribution of cross-boundary costs for the specified channel, window, and position;<br>result_ceiling=Limited to tested channels and outcomes. Do not infer J-axis jurisdiction or authority to act from spatial extent, organizational scope, or scope of influence.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Boundary selectivity. 2. Interface accessibility. 3. Cross-boundary costs</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Equating a boundary with closure<br>2. Equating membership status with consent<br>3. Equating scope of influence with jurisdiction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual cross-boundary events, cases of interface use, populations by membership category, distributions of admission and refusal, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers physical points of entry, organizational boundaries, digital interfaces, legal jurisdictions, and cross-domain channels. T=Time span covers the period when a boundary is in force, waiting at interfaces, migration lags, and cycles of reorganization. O=Organizational level covers user roles, gatekeeping teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers cross-boundary events, mechanisms of gatekeeping interaction, interface structure, institutional rules, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original admission and refusal records, sequences of use, individual cases, flow distributions, service metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers direct users, people excluded, positions where benefits or costs occur indirectly, second-order spillovers, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers interface nodes, gatekeeping bottlenecks, alternative paths, and cross-domain connections. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for inclusion, exclusion, interface changes, and adjustments of rights and responsibilities. Scale elevation requires differences to be recorded axis by axis</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>A boundary that selectively affects flows of resources, information, rights, or responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. Positions inside and outside. 2. Cross-boundary channels. 3. Limits of rights and responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. New membership categories<br>2. Interfaces across domains<br>3. Representation and authorization<br>4. Carryover of protections</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Membership, interfaces, and the boundaries of actual control can change</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Nonhuman boundaries without concepts of membership, rights, or responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Treating greater spatial or organizational scope as automatically conferring jurisdiction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Open<br>2. Selectively open<br>3. Closed<br>4. Disputed<br>5. Reorganizing</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Membership eligibility and decisions on admission and exit<br>2. Cross-boundary flows of resources, information, rights, and responsibilities<br>3. Gatekeeping nodes, wait times, and reasons for refusal<br>4. Differences between formal boundaries and boundaries of actual passage</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Membership lists and exceptions<br>2. Records of interface use<br>3. Records of cross-boundary flows and refusals</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. The HV01 candidate object or supported structural domain and its evidential status<br>2. Roles and authorization. R0 does not require G1 to pass first</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. HV05 uptake<br>2. HV07 write-back<br>3. PF-9 exit</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Record lags in boundaries taking effect or changing and in exit and appeal</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record informal boundaries, access through representatives, and drift in digital spaces</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Positions without access to the interface, without an exit, or withheld from disclosure for protection</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. Members<br>2. Applicants<br>3. People excluded<br>4. People bearing burdens outside the boundary</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Membership rules<br>2. Access mechanisms<br>3. Legal or institutional boundaries<br>4. Technical interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Boundary setters. 2. Interface operators. 3. Parties granting authorization</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>Facts about a boundary are separate from its normative justification</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>Up to the diagnostic level when evidence about interfaces and impacts is sufficient</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only descriptions of boundary states, interface barriers, exclusion risks, and testing needs. It does not authorize changes to admission, exit, rights, or resource flows. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. The formal membership boundary is the opposite of the boundary of actual resource control<br>2. A digital interface is open, but physical, language, or safety barriers prevent some positions from gaining access</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, affected people inside and outside the boundary can challenge inclusion, exclusion, and interface barriers through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original decision chain for the boundary</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline correct membership and refusal records, actually restore affected admission rights or interface states, and reverse erroneous boundary actions. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),o(t,{section:"A.2"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(d,[["render",l]]);export{g as __pageData,y as default};
